[2026-03-04T21:26:37.430Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GrK9iHuFEpEEWCWjBmjAjC","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# deploy\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→\n     5→Creates and/or syncs the production Neon database and creates and/or updates the Netlify site.\n     6→This is the single command for deploying the app to production. All project information\n     7→(database URL, Netlify site ID, etc.) is persisted in `.env` so subsequent runs reuse existing\n     8→resources.\n     9→\n    10→## Usage\n    11→\n    12→- `package.json` entry: `\"deploy\": \"tsx scripts/deploy.ts\"`\n    13→- No command-line arguments.\n    14→- Example: `npm run deploy`\n    15→\n    16→## Behavior\n    17→\n    18→### Database setup (first run)\n    19→\n    20→1. Check `.env` for an existing `NEON_PROJECT_ID`. If not present:\n    21→   a. Create a new Neon project via the Neon API.\n    22→   b. Write `NEON_PROJECT_ID` and `DATABASE_URL` to `.env`.\n    23→\n    24→### Database schema sync (every run)\n    25→\n    26→2. Call `initSchema(productionDatabaseUrl)` to create any missing tables.\n    27→3. Run migrations (`migrate-db` logic) against the production database to handle `ALTER TABLE`\n    28→   changes that `initSchema` cannot detect.\n    29→\n    30→### Seed data (first deployment only)\n    31→\n    32→4. On the **first deployment** (i.e. the database was just created), insert seed data that\n    33→   demonstrates the app's features. The seed data should be realistic and cover the main\n    34→   entities and relationships so a user can immediately explore the app without needing to\n    35→   create everything from scratch. Skip seeding on subsequent deployments.\n    36→\n    37→### Netlify site setup (first run)\n    38→\n    39→5. Check `.env` for an existing `NETLIFY_SITE_ID`. If not present:\n    40→   a. Create a new Netlify site via `netlify sites:create`.\n    41→   b. Write `NETLIFY_SITE_ID` to `.env`.\n    42→\n    43→### Build and deploy (every run)\n    44→\n    45→6. Build the app (`vite build`). Pipe build output to the log file.\n    46→7. Deploy to Netlify (`netlify deploy --prod`). Pipe deploy output to the log file.\n    47→8. Write the deployed URL, `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and `database_url` to the top of `deployment.txt`\n    48→   (overwriting the previous resource block but preserving any deployment history entries below).\n    49→9. Print a one-line summary to stdout:\n    50→   - Success: `Deployed to <url>`\n    51→   - Failure: `Deploy failed (build|netlify) — see logs/deploy.log`\n    52→\n    53→## Populating `.env` for Redeployments\n    54→\n    55→`.env` is gitignored and will not exist in a fresh environment. If the app has been\n    56→deployed before, you MUST populate `.env` before running the script so it reuses the\n    57→existing Netlify site and Neon database instead of creating new ones.\n    58→\n    59→Read `deployment.txt` (committed to git) to get all previously stored deployment\n    60→values (`site_id`, `neon_project_id`, `database_url`). Write them to `.env`:\n    61→\n    62→```\n    63→NETLIFY_SITE_ID=<site_id from deployment.txt>\n    64→NEON_PROJECT_ID=<neon_project_id from deployment.txt>\n    65→DATABASE_URL=<database_url from deployment.txt>\n    66→```\n    67→\n    68→If `.env` is missing these values the script will create **new** resources, which means\n    69→a new URL and an empty database. Always check `deployment.txt` first.\n    70→\n    71→## Inputs\n    72→\n    73→- **Environment variables**:\n    74→  - `NEON_API_KEY` (required): For Neon project/database management.\n    75→  - `NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN` (required): For Netlify CLI authentication.\n    76→  - `NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG` (required): For Netlify site creation.\n    77→- **Files**:\n    78→  - `.env`: Read for existing project info (`NEON_PROJECT_ID`, `DATABASE_URL`,\n    79→    `NETLIFY_SITE_ID`). Written to on first run.\n    80→  - `deployment.txt`: Resource block at the top contains `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and\n    81→    `database_url` from the last deployment (committed to git). Use this to populate `.env`\n    82→    when deploying in a fresh environment. Deployment history entries follow below.\n    83→\n    84→## Outputs\n    85→\n    86→- **stdout**: One-line summary only.\n    87→- **`logs/deploy.log`**: Full build and deploy output. Overwritten each run.\n    88→- **`.env`**: Updated with `NEON_PROJECT_ID`, `DATABASE_URL`, `NETLIFY_SITE_ID` if created.\n    89→- **`deployment.txt`**: Resource block at the top is updated with the current deployed URL,\n    90→  `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and `database_url`. Deployment history entries below the\n    91→  resource block are preserved. The deployment skill appends a new history entry after\n    92→  each successful deploy.\n    93→- **Side effects**:\n    94→  - Creates Neon project (first run only).\n    95→  - Syncs production database schema (every run).\n    96→  - Creates Netlify site (first run only).\n    97→  - Deploys built app to Netlify (every run).\n    98→- **Exit codes**:\n    99→  - 0: Deployment succeeded.\n   100→  - Non-zero: A step failed.\n   101→\n   102→## Locale Workaround\n   103→\n   104→The Netlify CLI requires a valid locale. In containers that lack `en_US.UTF-8`, CLI commands\n   105→(`netlify deploy`, `netlify sites:create`) fail with locale errors. Always prefix Netlify CLI\n   106→commands with `LC_ALL=C` to avoid this:\n   107→\n   108→```bash\n   109→LC_ALL=C npx netlify deploy --prod --dir dist --functions ./netlify/functions\n   110→LC_ALL=C npx netlify sites:create --account-slug $NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG\n   111→```\n   112→\n   113→The deploy script should set `LC_ALL=C` in the environment before spawning Netlify CLI\n   114→subprocesses.\n   115→\n   116→## Implementation Tips\n   117→\n   118→- Reuse `initSchema` from `scripts/schema.ts` for schema sync.\n   119→- Reuse migration logic from `scripts/migrate-db.ts`.\n   120→- Use the Neon REST API (`https://console.neon.tech/api/v2/...`) with `NEON_API_KEY` for\n   121→  project creation.\n   122→- Use `netlify sites:create --account-slug $NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG` for site creation.\n   123→- Use `netlify deploy --prod --dir dist --functions ./netlify/functions` for deployment.\n   124→- Do NOT inherit stdio from subprocesses. Pipe all subprocess output to `logs/deploy.log`.\n   125→- Read/write `.env` using `fs` — parse as key=value lines, append new entries, don't\n   126→  clobber existing values.\n   127→- Production builds must use `sourcemap: true`, `minify: false`, and the React development\n   128→  bundle (see `vite.config.ts` settings) so Replay recordings show readable source.\n   129→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43acca73-72ab-4f4f-9bc5-24081ded03a4","uuid":"7d6afebc-e489-4bea-8058-c30c7865b0b7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/scripts/deploy.md","content":"# deploy\n\n## Purpose\n\nCreates and/or syncs the production Neon database and creates and/or updates the Netlify site.\nThis is the single command for deploying the app to production. All project information\n(database URL, Netlify site ID, etc.) is persisted in `.env` so subsequent runs reuse existing\nresources.\n\n## Usage\n\n- `package.json` entry: `\"deploy\": \"tsx scripts/deploy.ts\"`\n- No command-line arguments.\n- Example: `npm run deploy`\n\n## Behavior\n\n### Database setup (first run)\n\n1. Check `.env` for an existing `NEON_PROJECT_ID`. If not present:\n   a. Create a new Neon project via the Neon API.\n   b. Write `NEON_PROJECT_ID` and `DATABASE_URL` to `.env`.\n\n### Database schema sync (every run)\n\n2. Call `initSchema(productionDatabaseUrl)` to create any missing tables.\n3. Run migrations (`migrate-db` logic) against the production database to handle `ALTER TABLE`\n   changes that `initSchema` cannot detect.\n\n### Seed data (first deployment only)\n\n4. On the **first deployment** (i.e. the database was just created), insert seed data that\n   demonstrates the app's features. The seed data should be realistic and cover the main\n   entities and relationships so a user can immediately explore the app without needing to\n   create everything from scratch. Skip seeding on subsequent deployments.\n\n### Netlify site setup (first run)\n\n5. Check `.env` for an existing `NETLIFY_SITE_ID`. If not present:\n   a. Create a new Netlify site via `netlify sites:create`.\n   b. Write `NETLIFY_SITE_ID` to `.env`.\n\n### Build and deploy (every run)\n\n6. Build the app (`vite build`). Pipe build output to the log file.\n7. Deploy to Netlify (`netlify deploy --prod`). Pipe deploy output to the log file.\n8. Write the deployed URL, `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and `database_url` to the top of `deployment.txt`\n   (overwriting the previous resource block but preserving any deployment history entries below).\n9. Print a one-line summary to stdout:\n   - Success: `Deployed to <url>`\n   - Failure: `Deploy failed (build|netlify) — see logs/deploy.log`\n\n## Populating `.env` for Redeployments\n\n`.env` is gitignored and will not exist in a fresh environment. If the app has been\ndeployed before, you MUST populate `.env` before running the script so it reuses the\nexisting Netlify site and Neon database instead of creating new ones.\n\nRead `deployment.txt` (committed to git) to get all previously stored deployment\nvalues (`site_id`, `neon_project_id`, `database_url`). Write them to `.env`:\n\n```\nNETLIFY_SITE_ID=<site_id from deployment.txt>\nNEON_PROJECT_ID=<neon_project_id from deployment.txt>\nDATABASE_URL=<database_url from deployment.txt>\n```\n\nIf `.env` is missing these values the script will create **new** resources, which means\na new URL and an empty database. Always check `deployment.txt` first.\n\n## Inputs\n\n- **Environment variables**:\n  - `NEON_API_KEY` (required): For Neon project/database management.\n  - `NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN` (required): For Netlify CLI authentication.\n  - `NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG` (required): For Netlify site creation.\n- **Files**:\n  - `.env`: Read for existing project info (`NEON_PROJECT_ID`, `DATABASE_URL`,\n    `NETLIFY_SITE_ID`). Written to on first run.\n  - `deployment.txt`: Resource block at the top contains `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and\n    `database_url` from the last deployment (committed to git). Use this to populate `.env`\n    when deploying in a fresh environment. Deployment history entries follow below.\n\n## Outputs\n\n- **stdout**: One-line summary only.\n- **`logs/deploy.log`**: Full build and deploy output. Overwritten each run.\n- **`.env`**: Updated with `NEON_PROJECT_ID`, `DATABASE_URL`, `NETLIFY_SITE_ID` if created.\n- **`deployment.txt`**: Resource block at the top is updated with the current deployed URL,\n  `site_id`, `neon_project_id`, and `database_url`. Deployment history entries below the\n  resource block are preserved. The deployment skill appends a new history entry after\n  each successful deploy.\n- **Side effects**:\n  - Creates Neon project (first run only).\n  - Syncs production database schema (every run).\n  - Creates Netlify site (first run only).\n  - Deploys built app to Netlify (every run).\n- **Exit codes**:\n  - 0: Deployment succeeded.\n  - Non-zero: A step failed.\n\n## Locale Workaround\n\nThe Netlify CLI requires a valid locale. In containers that lack `en_US.UTF-8`, CLI commands\n(`netlify deploy`, `netlify sites:create`) fail with locale errors. Always prefix Netlify CLI\ncommands with `LC_ALL=C` to avoid this:\n\n```bash\nLC_ALL=C npx netlify deploy --prod --dir dist --functions ./netlify/functions\nLC_ALL=C npx netlify sites:create --account-slug $NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG\n```\n\nThe deploy script should set `LC_ALL=C` in the environment before spawning Netlify CLI\nsubprocesses.\n\n## Implementation Tips\n\n- Reuse `initSchema` from `scripts/schema.ts` for schema sync.\n- Reuse migration logic from `scripts/migrate-db.ts`.\n- Use the Neon REST API (`https://console.neon.tech/api/v2/...`) with `NEON_API_KEY` for\n  project creation.\n- Use `netlify sites:create --account-slug $NETLIFY_ACCOUNT_SLUG` for site creation.\n- Use `netlify deploy --prod --dir dist --functions ./netlify/functions` for deployment.\n- Do NOT inherit stdio from subprocesses. Pipe all subprocess output to `logs/deploy.log`.\n- Read/write `.env` using `fs` — parse as key=value lines, append new entries, don't\n  clobber existing values.\n- Production builds must use `sourcemap: true`, `minify: false`, and the React development\n  bundle (see `vite.config.ts` settings) so Replay recordings show readable source.\n","numLines":129,"startLine":1,"totalLines":129}}}
