[2026-03-04T21:28:13.239Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NarrEbuU43q8mT9Geeu5m9","type":"tool_result","content":"# Setting Up Replay with Playwright\n\n## Installation Steps\n\nBegin by installing the Replay Playwright plugin using your preferred package manager:\n\n```sh\nnpm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright\n```\n\nThen install the Replay Browser itself:\n\n```sh\nnpx replayio install\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\nGenerate an API key by creating a Test Suite Team at https://app.replay.io/team/new/tests. Store this key in a `.env` file as `REPLAY_API_KEY=<your_api_key>` (recommended), or export it as an environment variable.\n\n## Configuration\n\nUpdate your `playwright.config.ts` to incorporate Replay functionality:\n\n```typescript\nimport { replayReporter, devices as replayDevices } from \"@replayio/playwright\";\n\nconst config: PlaywrightTestConfig = {\n  reporter: [\n    replayReporter({\n      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY,\n      upload: true,\n    }),\n    [\"line\"],\n  ],\n  projects: [\n    {\n      name: \"replay-chromium\",\n      use: { ...replayDevices[\"Replay Chromium\"] },\n    },\n  ],\n};\n```\n\nThe key configuration point: \"the `replayReporter` handles uploading recordings after test runs.\" Set `upload: true` to enable automatic uploads upon completion.\n\n## Running Tests\n\nExecute tests using the Replay Browser:\n\n```sh\nnpx playwright test --project replay-chromium\n```\n\nRecordings upload automatically when the reporter configuration includes `upload: true`. Preserve existing projects and reporters—add Replay components alongside them rather than replacing them."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43acca73-72ab-4f4f-9bc5-24081ded03a4","uuid":"36598787-e1bc-48fc-9364-f30fba01efbd","tool_use_result":{"bytes":5167,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Setting Up Replay with Playwright\n\n## Installation Steps\n\nBegin by installing the Replay Playwright plugin using your preferred package manager:\n\n```sh\nnpm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright\n```\n\nThen install the Replay Browser itself:\n\n```sh\nnpx replayio install\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\nGenerate an API key by creating a Test Suite Team at https://app.replay.io/team/new/tests. Store this key in a `.env` file as `REPLAY_API_KEY=<your_api_key>` (recommended), or export it as an environment variable.\n\n## Configuration\n\nUpdate your `playwright.config.ts` to incorporate Replay functionality:\n\n```typescript\nimport { replayReporter, devices as replayDevices } from \"@replayio/playwright\";\n\nconst config: PlaywrightTestConfig = {\n  reporter: [\n    replayReporter({\n      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY,\n      upload: true,\n    }),\n    [\"line\"],\n  ],\n  projects: [\n    {\n      name: \"replay-chromium\",\n      use: { ...replayDevices[\"Replay Chromium\"] },\n    },\n  ],\n};\n```\n\nThe key configuration point: \"the `replayReporter` handles uploading recordings after test runs.\" Set `upload: true` to enable automatic uploads upon completion.\n\n## Running Tests\n\nExecute tests using the Replay Browser:\n\n```sh\nnpx playwright test --project replay-chromium\n```\n\nRecordings upload automatically when the reporter configuration includes `upload: true`. Preserve existing projects and reporters—add Replay components alongside them rather than replacing them.","durationMs":3783,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-playwright/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-04T21:28:13.802Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W46bbXLYsdpuutrPoChhid","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay MCP Tools: Analysis Instructions\n\n## Core Methodology\n\nFollow this four-step framework when investigating recordings:\n\n1. **Formulate a question** about the app's behavior\n2. **Explore** using discovery tools to identify relevant events\n3. **Explain** using analysis tools to understand causation\n4. **Conclude** by connecting findings to your hypothesis\n\n## Exploratory Tools\n\n**Errors**: Identify `UncaughtException` and `ReactException` events that may break functionality.\n\n**Timeline**: Use `Timeline`, `ConsoleMessages`, `LocalStorage`, `NetworkRequest`, and `ReactRenders` to sequence events chronologically.\n\n**Sources**: Employ `ListSources`, `ReadSource`, and `SearchSources` to locate and examine code execution patterns.\n\n## Explanatory Tools\n\n**Dependencies**: Use `GetStack` and `ControlDependency` to understand \"happens-before\" relationships between events.\n\n**Details**: Apply `DescribePoint`, `InspectElement`, `ReactComponents`, `Logpoint`, and `Evaluate` to examine state and variable values at specific moments.\n\n## Recommended Approach\n\nThe documentation emphasizes: \"when understanding JS behavior it is a good idea to find code relevant to the question being answered, see whether that code executed at all, and get details about the times when it executed.\"\n\nUse `Logpoint` to track expression values across executions and identify anomalies worth deeper investigation."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43acca73-72ab-4f4f-9bc5-24081ded03a4","uuid":"1353c9bc-113b-4124-9454-e8d93c2ef323","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay MCP Tools: Analysis Instructions\n\n## Core Methodology\n\nFollow this four-step framework when investigating recordings:\n\n1. **Formulate a question** about the app's behavior\n2. **Explore** using discovery tools to identify relevant events\n3. **Explain** using analysis tools to understand causation\n4. **Conclude** by connecting findings to your hypothesis\n\n## Exploratory Tools\n\n**Errors**: Identify `UncaughtException` and `ReactException` events that may break functionality.\n\n**Timeline**: Use `Timeline`, `ConsoleMessages`, `LocalStorage`, `NetworkRequest`, and `ReactRenders` to sequence events chronologically.\n\n**Sources**: Employ `ListSources`, `ReadSource`, and `SearchSources` to locate and examine code execution patterns.\n\n## Explanatory Tools\n\n**Dependencies**: Use `GetStack` and `ControlDependency` to understand \"happens-before\" relationships between events.\n\n**Details**: Apply `DescribePoint`, `InspectElement`, `ReactComponents`, `Logpoint`, and `Evaluate` to examine state and variable values at specific moments.\n\n## Recommended Approach\n\nThe documentation emphasizes: \"when understanding JS behavior it is a good idea to find code relevant to the question being answered, see whether that code executed at all, and get details about the times when it executed.\"\n\nUse `Logpoint` to track expression values across executions and identify anomalies worth deeper investigation.","durationMs":3432,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md"}}
